arm64: dts: mediatek: gemini: record what i2c6's failure is NOT

i2c6's address phase works and its data phase does not (B-45, #57), and that
bus stands between this machine and its own clock speed: the DA9214's bucks
cannot be identified until it works, the CPU regulator cannot be declared
until they are, and cpufreq cannot bind until the regulator exists.

This patch changes no setting. It records eliminations, because #57's two
named candidates are now both dead and the entry should not send the next
session down them again.

Measured afresh on #43, and it is sharper than B-45 had it: every address on
the bus NACKs correctly — 0x08, 0x20, 0x40, 0x50, 0x55, 0x60, 0x67, 0x6c,
0x77 all fail — except 0x68-0x6b, which ACK *and accept byte writes*. So a
slave really is there and the master really is working. What fails is only the
read data phase: a constant 0x00 at all four addresses, forty reads out of
forty.

A trip to stock Android on boot1 read the vendor's own device tree and live
pin state:

  * clock-div — DEAD. The vendor's i2c@1100e000 has clock-div = <0x0a>, and
    so does every other i2c controller in its DTB. So do we.
  * pinmux — DEAD. The vendor has GPIO151 and GPIO152 both in mode 1, which
    is SCL6_0/SDA6_0, exactly what i2c6_pins_a selects. Our own mode
    registers (0x10005420 bits[31:28], 0x10005430 bits[3:0]) read 0x1 for
    both pins and pinmux-pins shows them claimed by 1100e000.i2c, func1.
  * "open-drain on a bus with no pull-ups", which is what dts/0043's comment
    offered as the reason every register reads 0x00 — RETRACTED. Both pads
    read DIN = 1 on our kernel (GPIO DI at 0x10005240, bits 23 and 24):
    idle-high, exactly like the working i2c7 pads beside them. There are
    pull-ups and the bus is not stuck low.
  * the controller's timing configuration generally — DEAD. i2c7 has the
    same driver data, the same clock-div and the same 156 MHz source clock,
    and reads its RT5735 ten times out of ten.
  * the bus speed — TRIED ON #44 AND RETRACTED. The vendor runs this
    controller at 3400000 (0x0033E140, high-speed mode, matching I2C_HS_FLAG
    and timing = 3400 in the vendor's own
    drivers/misc/mediatek/power/mt6797/da9214.c) against our inherited
    400000, and it was the last configuration difference left. It does not
    fix it, and it makes it slightly worse: at 3.4 MHz the reads that used to
    return 0x00 mostly fail outright instead. 400 kHz is kept for that
    reason, not because it is known to be right.

What is left is not a setting we have found. The live clue is that reads are
not uniformly dead: 0x00 nearly always, but occasionally a real-looking byte
(0x0b and 0xd6 on #42, 0x05 on #44). A bus that loses most bits but not all
is not an unpowered slave and not a wrong mux; it looks like contention. This
controller is the one the vendor marks `mediatek,appm_used`, and mainline
i2c-mt65xx knows nothing about that property. If a hardware power-management
engine also masters this bus, our transfers would be colliding with its.
That is the next hypothesis and it is untested.


UPDATE: the cause was found in the vendor driver, not in this node. MT6797's
APPM controller packs both write-then-read lengths into TRANSFER_LEN, and
mainline was writing the read length to TRANSFER_LEN_AUX, leaving it zero —
address phase fine, data phase empty, every register 0x00. Fixed in
i2c/0001. This node now also carries `mediatek,appm_used`, which is how the
driver knows which controller that is; the vendor DTB carries it here and
nowhere else.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
@@ -750,36 +750,60 @@
 &i2c6 {
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 	pinctrl-0 = <&i2c6_pins_a>;
-	clock-frequency = <400000>;
 	/*
-	 * This bus is push-pull on this board, and without saying so every
-	 * register on the DA9214 reads back 0x00.
+	 * The symptom (B-45, #57): every address on this bus NACKs correctly
+	 * except 0x68-0x6b, which ACK and accept byte writes — so a slave IS
+	 * there — yet every register read returns 0x00 at all four addresses.
+	 * The address phase works and the data phase does not.
 	 *
-	 * The symptom is worth describing because it looks exactly like an
-	 * absent or dead chip and was read as one: i2cdetect finds 0x68-0x6b
-	 * (the DA9214's four addresses) and every one of them ACKs, but data
-	 * reads return 0x00, or intermittently fail outright with ENXIO. That
-	 * is what open-drain on a bus with no pull-ups does — the slave can
-	 * still pull the line low to ACK, so addressing works, while the data
-	 * phase never gets a clean high and every bit reads back as zero.
+	 * What the boot1 Android trip on 2026-08-21 eliminated, by reading the
+	 * vendor's own device tree and pin state:
 	 *
-	 * It is also what made B-43 conclude "there is NO DA9214 on this
-	 * board", from mainline da9211 reading DEVICE_ID as 0x0. Stock Android
-	 * on boot1 has the part bound to its own `da9214` driver on this exact
-	 * bus and address, and its device tree carries
-	 * `mediatek,use-push-pull` on i2c@1100e000. We did not.
+	 *   * clock-div. Vendor i2c@1100e000 has clock-div = <0x0a>. So do we.
+	 *   * pinmux. The vendor has GPIO151/GPIO152 in mode 1, which is
+	 *     SCL6_0/SDA6_0 — exactly what i2c6_pins_a selects, and our own
+	 *     mode register reads 0x1 for both pins.
+	 *   * pull-ups / a stuck bus. Both pads read DIN = 1 on our kernel
+	 *     (GPIO DI 0x10005240 bits 23,24), idle-high like the working
+	 *     i2c7 pads next to them. This also retires the reasoning that
+	 *     used to sit in this comment, which explained the 0x00 reads as
+	 *     "open-drain on a bus with no pull-ups". There are pull-ups.
+	 *   * controller timing generally. i2c7 has the same driver data, the
+	 *     same clock-div, and the same 156 MHz source clock, and it reads
+	 *     its RT5735 perfectly.
 	 *
-	 * Mainline i2c-mt65xx honours the property (it writes
-	 * I2C_IO_CONFIG_PUSH_PULL to OFFSET_IO_CONFIG at bus init), so this is
-	 * a one-property fix, and it is safe here: i2c6 has exactly one slave
-	 * and one master.
+	 *   * the bus speed, tried on #44 and RETRACTED. The vendor's
+	 *     clock-frequency here is 3400000 (0x0033E140 — high-speed mode,
+	 *     matching I2C_HS_FLAG and timing = 3400 in the vendor's
+	 *     drivers/misc/mediatek/power/mt6797/da9214.c) against our
+	 *     inherited 400000, and it was the last configuration difference
+	 *     left. It does not fix it and it makes it slightly worse: at
+	 *     3.4 MHz the reads that used to return 0x00 mostly fail outright.
+	 *     400 kHz is kept for that reason, not because it is right.
 	 *
-	 * The vendor additionally drives this part in high-speed mode at
-	 * 3.4 MHz (`I2C_HS_FLAG`, `timing = 3400` in
-	 * drivers/misc/mediatek/power/mt6797/da9214.c). 400 kHz is kept until
-	 * there is a reason to need more; register access does not.
+	 * What is left is not a setting we have found. The live clue is that
+	 * reads are not uniformly dead — 0x00 nearly always, but occasionally
+	 * a real-looking byte (0x0b, 0xd6 on #42; 0x05 on #44). A bus that
+	 * loses most bits but not all is not an unpowered slave and not a
+	 * wrong mux; it looks like contention. Note this controller is the
+	 * one the vendor marks `mediatek,appm_used`, and mainline i2c-mt65xx
+	 * knows nothing about that property — if a hardware power-management
+	 * engine also masters this bus, our transfers would be colliding with
+	 * its. That is the next hypothesis to test and it is untested.
 	 */
+	clock-frequency = <400000>;
+	/* Vendor DTB carries this on i2c@1100e000; one master, one slave. */
 	mediatek,use-push-pull;
+	/*
+	 * This is the APPM controller — the one the CPU DVFS co-processor
+	 * shares — and on MT6797 that changes how a write-then-read length is
+	 * programmed: both halves go in TRANSFER_LEN, not TRANSFER_LEN_AUX.
+	 * The vendor DTB carries this property here and nowhere else, and its
+	 * driver keys the packed-length path off exactly it. Without it every
+	 * register on this bus reads back 0x00 while the address phase works,
+	 * which is the whole of B-45.
+	 */
+	mediatek,appm_used;
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
